# Doxia Sitetools - SCM Context

This optional module adds the date and author of the last change to each page, read from the version control system
of the site sources. A skin can then show "Last updated" information per page instead of one publish date for the
whole site.

${esc.h}${esc.h} Setup

Add the module to the dependencies of the Maven Site Plugin. It needs Maven Site Plugin 3.22.0 or later, the first
release built on Doxia Sitetools 2.1.0.

```xml
<plugin>
  <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
  <artifactId>maven-site-plugin</artifactId>
  <dependencies>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.apache.maven.doxia</groupId>
      <artifactId>doxia-site-scm-context</artifactId>
      <version>${project.version}</version>
    </dependency>
  </dependencies>
</plugin>
```

${esc.h}${esc.h} What it adds

For each rendered document the module adds two variables to the Velocity context:

| Variable | Type | Description |
|:---|:---|:---|
| `scmModifiedDate` | `Date` | The date of the last commit that changed the source file. |
| `scmModifiedAuthor` | `String` | The author of that commit. |

When a page does not set an author or a date itself, the site template variables `authors` and `documentDate` fall
back to these values; see the [Site Template section](../doxia-site-renderer/index.html${esc.hash}Site_Template) of the
Site Renderer. Whether and how the values appear on the page is up to the skin.

${esc.h}${esc.h} How it works

The module is a [`ContextCustomizer`](../doxia-site-renderer/apidocs/org/apache/maven/doxia/siterenderer/ContextCustomizer.html).
On the first document it looks for a working copy in the site root directory and then in its parent directories,
using the [Maven SCM](/scm/) providers for Git, Mercurial and Subversion. It then asks the SCM for the last change of
each source file, one query per document. If no working copy is found, nothing is added and the site renders as
before.

A shallow Git clone has no history beyond its last commits, so the dates would be wrong; the module logs a warning
when the repository is shallow. On CI, check out the full history, for example with `fetch-depth: 0` in GitHub
Actions' `actions/checkout`.

Generated documents, such as reports, have no source file and get no SCM values.
